\f0\b\fs46 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The voicing effect on preceding vowel duration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs34 \cf0 This is one of the most robust and heavily studied phenomena in phonetics: vowels (and other segments) preceding voiced obstruents are consistently longer than the "same" vowel preceding voiceless obstruents. In English the effect is large \'97 commonly cited ratios are around 1.5:1 (sometimes up to 1.8:1), e.g., "bead" vs. "beat," "bag" vs. "back."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs38 \cf0 Key empirical literature\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs34 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 House & Fairbanks (1953)
\f1\b0  and 
\f0\b Peterson & Lehiste (1960)
\f1\b0  gave some of the earliest careful acoustic measurements in English, establishing that vowel duration varies systematically with the voicing of the following consonant, more than with almost any other segmental factor besides stress.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Chen (1970)
\f1\b0 , "Vowel length variation as a function of the voicing of the consonant environment," is the classic cross-linguistic survey. Chen found the effect in English, French, Russian, and Korean, but with very different magnitudes \'97 largest in English, much smaller in French and Russian. This established that the effect, while widespread, is not a fixed universal constant but is 
\f2\i phonetically motivated and phonologically variable
\f1\i0 .\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Lisker (1974)
\f1\b0  pushed back against a purely mechanical explanation, pointing out that if the effect were strictly aerodynamically forced, its magnitude shouldn't differ so much across languages with similar consonant inventories. This helped reframe the debate as mechanical bias vs. language-specific phonologization.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Raphael (1972)
\f1\b0  and later 
\f0\b Kluender, Diehl & Wright (1988)
\f1\b0  and 
\f0\b Summers (1987)
\f1\b0  turned to perception, showing that listeners use preceding vowel duration as a strong cue to coda obstruent voicing, sometimes even overriding weak or absent voicing cues in the consonant itself (especially with unreleased or devoiced word-final stops).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Port & Dalby (1982)
\f1\b0  looked at how this interacts with speaking rate and closure duration, supporting a timing/compensation account.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Sol\'e9 (2007)
\f1\b0  and related work situate the effect within the broader aerodynamic voicing constraint literature (building on 
\f0\b Ohala
\f1\b0 ), asking which durational effects are "automatic" phonetic responses vs. which have been phonologized into language-specific timing rules.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs38 \cf0 Why it happens: the competing/complementary explanations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf0 1. Aerodynamic voicing maintenance (laryngeal adjustment account)
\f1\b0  Voicing requires a sustained transglottal pressure drop \'97 air must keep flowing from the lungs across the vibrating vocal folds. During an oral closure, however, supraglottal pressure rises and that pressure drop shrinks, which tends to extinguish voicing on its own (the 
\f2\i aerodynamic voicing constraint
\f1\i0 , Ohala). To produce a "truly" voiced stop or fricative, speakers must actively counteract this: enlarging the oral cavity (lowering the larynx, advancing the tongue root, slackening the pharyngeal/cheek walls) and/or making laryngeal adjustments (slackening the vocal folds) to keep the pressure differential large enough for voicing to continue through the closure. These adjustments (Halle & Stevens 1967 discuss the laryngeal side) take articulatory time and often begin to be implemented before the closure itself, spilling backward into the preceding vowel and altering its timing.\

\f0\b 2. Compensatory closure-duration / timing account
\f1\b0  Voiceless obstruents, especially stops, tend to be produced with more articulatory force and have inherently longer, more clearly-defined closure durations than their voiced counterparts (voiced stops' closures are shortened partly 
\f2\i because
\f1\i0  shorter closure = less time for the pressure to equalize = easier to sustain voicing, per point 1). If the overall rhyme (vowel + coda) is subject to some temporal compensation or near-isochrony, a longer consonant closure "steals" time from the vowel, and a shorter one leaves more time available to the vowel \'97 yielding a shorter vowel before voiceless codas and a longer one before voiced codas. Chen and later Port & Dalby's rate studies are consistent with this kind of trading relationship.\

\f0\b 3. Perceptual enhancement account
\f1\b0  Kluender, Diehl & Wright (1988) argued that because coda-consonant voicing cues are often acoustically weak or ambiguous in production (final stops are frequently unreleased, only weakly voiced during closure, etc.), speakers exploit \'97 and possibly actively exaggerate \'97 vowel duration as an additional, robust perceptual cue to compensate. On this view the durational difference isn't purely a mechanical byproduct but is at least partly shaped by communicative pressure to keep the voicing contrast perceptually salient even when the segment itself under-differentiates it.\

\f0\b 4. Reconciling universality vs. variability
\f1\b0  The current consensus, following Chen and Sol\'e9's cross-linguistic comparisons, is roughly: the aerodynamic/articulatory pressures in (1) and (2) create a 
\f2\i natural phonetic bias
\f1\i0  toward this durational pattern in essentially any language with a voicing contrast in obstruents. But languages differ in how strongly they phonologize (grammaticalize) that bias into an obligatory, large-magnitude timing rule. English is a striking case of strong phonologization (partly reinforced, per (3), because English also frequently neutralizes or weakens final-obstruent voicing cues themselves, making the vowel-duration cue more functionally important). Languages with more robust coda voicing cues elsewhere (e.g., consistent closure voicing, releases) show a smaller vowel-duration effect because they don't need to lean on it as heavily.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs38 \cf0 Summary of causal chain\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs34 \cf0 Underlying aerodynamics (harder to sustain voicing under rising oral pressure) \uc0\u8594  shorter closures and articulatory adjustments for voiced obstruents \u8594  compensatory/coarticulatory lengthening of the preceding vowel \u8594  this durational difference becomes a salient, learnable acoustic cue \u8594  languages phonologize and sometimes amplify it to different degrees, with English being a particularly strong case, likely reinforced by the weakness of English's own coda-voicing cues.\
Happy to go deeper into any one strand \'97 e.g., the EMG/aerodynamic instrumental studies underlying the laryngeal-adjustment account, or the perceptual weighting literature (Summers 1987; Wardrip-Fruin 1982) on how listeners actually use this cue.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs46 \cf0 Stress and the voicing effect\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs34 \cf0 Stress interacts with the voicing effect in two related ways scholars have examined: (1) stress is itself a major independent determinant of vowel duration, so it has to be controlled for or factored out when studying the voicing effect, and (2) the 
\f2\i magnitude
\f1\i0  of the voicing effect itself appears to be modulated by stress \'97 it is generally reported to be smaller, and sometimes largely absent, in unstressed syllables.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs38 \cf0 Stress as a durational factor in its own right\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs34 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Peterson & Lehiste (1960)
\f1\b0  and later 
\f0\b Klatt (1976)
\f1\b0 , in his influential model of segmental duration in English, treat stress as one of the primary hierarchical factors governing vowel and syllable duration, situated above finer effects like consonantal voicing. Klatt's durational rules essentially stack effects multiplicatively/additively: stress first sets a baseline duration, and then the following-consonant-voicing effect is applied as a further adjustment within that stressed or unstressed context. This ordering matters because it implies the voicing effect is not categorically independent of stress but operates "on top of" it.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Crystal & House (1988)
\f1\b0 , in their large-scale corpus study of American English segment durations, likewise found stress to be one of the strongest predictors of vowel duration, with the coda-voicing effect a smaller, secondary factor by comparison in raw magnitude, though still highly reliable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs38 \cf0 \strokec2 Attenuation of the voicing effect under reduced stress\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs34 \cf0 The more specific claim scholars make is that the voicing effect shrinks, and may even disappear, when the syllable is unstressed or destressed:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Chen (1970)
\f1\b0 , in the same cross-linguistic paper that established the voicing effect's variability across languages, also noted that within English the effect was most robust in stressed monosyllables and weaker in polysyllabic or less-stressed contexts, which he took as evidence that speakers have more articulatory "room" to implement the durational adjustment when the syllable already carries substantial duration and prominence.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Umeda (1975)
\f1\b0 , working with continuous/connected speech rather than isolated monosyllables, found that many of the durational effects well-documented in citation-form word lists \'97 including the voicing effect \'97 were considerably reduced or obscured in unstressed and phrase-medial contexts, where overall vowel shortening and reduction dominate.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Work in the 
\f0\b Klatt (1976)
\f1\b0  tradition and subsequent speech-synthesis-oriented duration modeling (e.g., 
\f0\b van Santen 1992
\f1\b0 ) generally builds in an interaction term rather than treating stress and coda voicing as simply additive, because purely additive models under-predict the small voicing effect actually observed in unstressed syllables and over-predict it if applied uniformly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs38 \cf0 \strokec2 Proposed explanations for the interaction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs34 \cf0 Two broad accounts appear in the literature for 
\f2\i why
\f1\i0  stress would modulate the voicing effect rather than the effects being independent:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Articulatory/aerodynamic account
\f1\b0 : Since the voicing effect is argued to originate partly in active laryngeal and supralaryngeal adjustments to sustain voicing through a following obstruent closure (see the earlier laryngeal-adjustment literature \'97 Halle & Stevens 1967), and since unstressed syllables are produced with generally reduced articulatory effort and more centralized, undershot gestures, there is simply less articulatory "budget" available to implement the compensatory timing adjustment in unstressed position. On this view the attenuation under destressing is a natural consequence of the same mechanical logic that produces the effect in the first place.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Perceptual/functional account
\f1\b0 : Following the Kluender, Diehl & Wright (1988) enhancement logic, if vowel duration is partly recruited as a cue to coda voicing because other voicing cues are weak, this cue-enhancement pressure may itself be weighted differently depending on prosodic prominence \'97 listeners may rely less on fine segmental cues in unstressed syllables generally (where whole-word or phrase-level cues take precedence), reducing the functional pressure to maintain a large durational contrast there.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs38 \cf0 \strokec2 Net picture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs34 \cf0 The consensus across this literature is that stress and coda-obstruent voicing are not independent durational factors that simply add together; rather, stress largely licenses or amplifies the space in which the voicing effect can be phonetically realized, so that the effect is most clearly observed in careful, stressed, often word-final or monosyllabic contexts (the classic "bead/beat" comparisons), and becomes progressively weaker, more variable, and harder to detect reliably as stress and prosodic prominence are reduced.\
If it's useful, I can go further into the specific quantitative duration-modeling literature (Klatt's rule system or van Santen's later work), which gives explicit numerical estimates of how much of the voicing effect "survives" under destressing.\
}